<% content_for :title, "About" %>

<article class="about">
  <header>
    <h1>About AI Music Tube</h1>
    <p class="about-intro">Discover music made with AI, and see what the technology actually did.</p>
  </header>

  <dl class="about-stats">
    <div><dt>YouTube uploads</dt><dd><%= number_with_delimiter @disclosure_counts.values.sum %></dd></div>
    <div><dt>Artists and channels</dt><dd><%= number_with_delimiter @artist_count %></dd></div>
    <div><dt><%= link_to "Catalog tags", tags_path, class: "text-link" %></dt><dd><%= number_with_delimiter @genre_count + Track::LABELS.size %></dd></div>
  </dl>
  <p class="stats-note">A song, album, or mix counts as one upload.</p>

  <section>
      <h2>Why this catalog exists</h2>
      <p>People talk a lot about how AI is changing software development, but I rarely hear anyone around me talk about AI-generated music or what it means for the music industry. I wanted to show why it’s a bigger deal than many of us realize: even with a trained ear, it’s not always easy to tell whether the music you’re hearing was made by a person or generated by AI.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Know an artist we’re missing?</h2>
    <p><%= link_to "Open a pull request on GitHub", artist_pull_request_url, class: "text-link" %> to suggest an artist. Include a link to their music on YouTube and a source for any AI music credit.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Why this catalog exists</h2>
    <p>On YouTube, an AI-made music video, a human recording with a restored vocal, and a song with generated vocals can look the same in the feed. AI Music Tube brings songs, albums, and long mixes together and explains what’s known about each recording.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Playback and your library</h2>
    <p>Select the play icon on a track to load the official YouTube player. If it won’t play here, try <strong>Watch on YouTube</strong>. AI Music Tube doesn’t host audio or video and isn’t affiliated with or endorsed by YouTube or Google.</p>
    <p>Likes, playlists, and history are tied to this browser. There’s no account or syncing, and clearing the site’s cookie removes access to your library. History records when you choose to play or open a track, not how long you listen.</p>
    <p><%= link_to "Most played", root_path(sort: "most_played"), class: "text-link" %> counts clicks on the player or on <strong>Watch on YouTube</strong> here, including repeat plays. These are site totals, not YouTube views or listening time. Clearing your history doesn’t change them.</p>
  </section>

  <p><%= link_to "Browse the catalog", root_path, class: "text-link" %></p>
</article>
